074, 75074], "confidence": 0.85, "evidence": [{"trace_id": "step_02", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_07", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_10", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_11", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_15", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_04", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_05", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_08", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_12", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_03", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_13", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_09", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_06", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_14", "role": "confounder_control"}], "narrative": "No regime switch. At the prescribed 5000 half-window, turnover peaks at just 0.33 (t=54689) with ~33 of ~40 contacts shared before and after, and the curve is a broad hump rather than a step. Low sub-window Jaccards (0.22, 0.00) reflect rotation of which group member is active against one fixed ~30-node partner roster (40,61,63,80,116,184,239,250,327,338,349,493,515,749,785,897,912,918,924,926,1110,1348,1355,1505,1526,1593,1750,1760,1792,1922), which every member shares across the entire window. The sharp 0.71 spike at bandwidth 2500 coincides with an activity void (2 group events in 59074-61073; global dip 612->282), so it is a sampling artifact, not a partner replacement. No bursts at z>=2 and evenly distributed per-node volume rule out volume- and popularity-driven explanations."}, "raw_final_response": "## Analysis\n\n**Group-level turnover is low and smooth.** At the requested half-window of 5000 (step_02), the change curve rises gently from 0.27 to a *peak of only 0.33* at t=54689 and then decays to 0.14\u20130.21 \u2014 a broad, rounded hump, not the near-1.0 spike a genuine old-set\u2192new-set replacement would produce. Crucially, the before/after contact lists at that peak are nearly identical: ~33 of ~40 partners (40, 61, 63, 80, 116, 184, 239, 250, 327, 338, 349, 493, 515, 749, 785, 897, 912, 918, 924, 926, 1110, 1348, 1355, 1505, 1526, 1593, 1750, 1760, 1792, 1922) are shared. Only a handful of one-off peripheral contacts differ.\n\n**The apparently dramatic diffs are a member-rotation artifact, not partner turnover.** Steps 04/05/08 report jaccard 0.22 \u2192 0.00 \u2192 0.00, which naively looks like total replacement. But reading the added/removed edge lists shows the *same* partner set being handed from one group member to another: `[182,40],[182,61],[182,63]...` removed while `[654,40],[654,61],[654,63]...` added; then 654 \u2192 731/959; then 114/140 \u2192 1268/1311. The edge endpoints on the group side change; the contact universe does not. Steps 07, 10, 11, 15 confirm this directly \u2014 1107, 114, 1268 and 356 each hold ~30\u201336 neighbours drawn from one identical core roster spanning the *whole* 30k window. Members take turns being active against a fixed audience.\n\n**Confounder controls.** (a) The sharp 2500-bandwidth spikes (0.66\u20130.71 at t\u224858471\u201361676, step_12) are not a real switch: they sit exactly on an activity void \u2014 the group logged just 2 events in bin 59074\u201361073 (step_03), and global traffic also dipped from 612 to 282 events (step_13). With almost no events, Jaccard is computed on a near-empty set, so the spike is a sampling artifact that vanishes at the prescribed 5000 bandwidth. (b) No bursts at z\u22652.0 (step_09); volume is flat (~55.5/bin, sd 16.95), so no abrupt event surge marks a transition. (c) Activity is evenly spread across members (90\u201396 events, 30\u201336 partners each, step_06), and static connectivity alone (step_14: 1268\u219240 exists) is not evidence of timing.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_8155967f\",\n  \"decision\": \"absent\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"regime_switch\",\n  \"entities\": [114, 140, 182, 293, 356, 654, 731, 959, 1107, 1260, 1268, 1311],\n  \"window\": [45074, 75074],\n  \"confidence\": 0.85,\n